9:BACKGROUND: Affective symptoms are a prevalent psychopathological feature in various psychiatric disorders. However, the underlying neurobiological mechanisms are complex and not yet fully understood.
11:METHODS: We used normative modeling to establish a reference for functional activation of functional magnetic resonance imaging based on an emotional episodic memory task, which is frequently used to study affective symptoms in psychiatric disorders. This normative reference was derived from a large dataset of healthy individuals ( n = 409) and used to evaluate individualized functional alterations by calculating deviations from this reference in a clinical dataset, which included 164 healthy control participants and patients with major depressive disorder (MDD) ( n = 56), bipolar disorder (BD) ( n = 31), and schizophrenia (SZ) ( n = 73). The functional deviations were mapped to emotional networks (ENs) with speci /uniFB01 c emotional functions and used to predict affective symptoms in different mental disorders. The microscale cellular signatures underlying macroscale variations were identi /uniFB01 ed using imaging transcriptomic analysis and associated with affective symptoms.
13:RESULTS: We observed distinct patterns of cross-scale neural alterations linked to affective symptoms in 3 psychiatric disorders. Macroscale neural dysfunctions in distinct disorders were embedded into non-overlapping ENs and signi /uniFB01 cantly associated with affective symptoms. Oligodendrocytes may mediate the network-speci /uniFB01 c impairments and microglia for MDD, astrocytes for BD, and excitatory neurons for SZ as replicable cell-type correlates of affective symptoms.
15:CONCLUSIONS: These /uniFB01 ndings revealed cross-scale neural alterations underlying affective symptoms in psychiatric disorders, providing a basis for understanding their neuropathological patterns and guiding individualized treatment.
19:Affective symptoms, a basic dimension of psychopathology, are highly prevalent across multiple psychiatric conditions (1). In clinical settings, affective symptoms in psychiatric patients manifest as disturbances in emotional processing, the result of which is emotional extremes in perception (2), attention (3), and response (4), and display signi /uniFB01 cant variability in distinct forms of clinical phenotype (5,6). Clarifying the neurobiological underpinnings related to psychiatric symptoms is a major challenge for contemporary research in psychiatry and would help advance the intervention optimization of psychiatric disorders (7 -9). However, the neurobiological alterations underlying affective symptoms in psychiatry are complex and remain poorly understood.
21:Functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) studies have been a major step forward in characterizing macroscale neural dysfunctions related to affective symptoms in psychiatric patients (5,10,11). The emotional episodic memory task (12 -14), a widely used paradigm, effectively captures functional activation associated with adaptive memory-enhancing effects induced by emotional stimuli (15 -17). Maladaptive alterations in this task have frequently been observed in major depressive disorder (MDD) (18,19), bipolar disorder (BD) (20,21), and schizophrenia (SZ) (22,23), suggesting the neural dysfunctions of affective perturbation. Disrupted emotional memory retrieval has been reported that may manifest in multiple processes of pathological emotion in psychiatric patients, involving disturbances in emotional attention (24), perceptions (25), and regulation (26). Importantly, these disruptions were thought to link to the vulnerability and development of speci /uniFB01 c clinical affective syndromes (27,28), underscoring the task ' s relevance in assessing affective symptoms.
25:Studies using emotional episodic memory tasks have identi/uniFB01 ed altered neurofunctions related to affective symptoms in the amygdala and medial temporal lobe (MTL) system across various mental disorders (29 -31), suggesting heterogeneous patterns of neural dysfunctions in distinct diagnoses (18,32 -34). However, inconsistent results have often been reported, both within one speci /uniFB01 c diagnosis and across disorders. In MDD, increased activity in the amygdala and orbitofrontal gyrus has often been associated with emotional bias (18,19), but another study has shown no abnormalities (33). Hypoactivation in the amygdala and occipital gyrus has been recognized in patients with BD compared with patients with MDD (20,35), although an earlier study reported increased activity (36). Individuals with SZ have shown both hyperactivation and hypoactivation in the prefrontal and parietal cortices (37 -39), accompanied by coactivation with the MTL areas (37,38,40). Furthermore, while a study has reported higher activation of the cingulate cortex in patients with BD relative to patients with SZ (34), others have reported the opposite (41).
27:Although these mixed /uniFB01 ndings could be partially due to differences in task design, one more major reason is the individual differences within and across clinical cohorts, which were often not captured by traditional case-control designs (42,43). To address this, an individualized approach that disentangles the intersubject heterogeneity of neural dysfunctions that underpin affective symptoms is therefore necessary. Normative modeling quanti /uniFB01 es each patient as an extreme deviation from normative expectations for MRI-derived phenotypes based on clinical or cognitive characteristics by incorporating neural alterations into a continuous spectrum derived from a large healthy population (44,45). This method shares similarities with other dimensional methods commonly used to predict clinical variables from neuroimaging data (46). By cutting across diagnostic classi /uniFB01 cations, this approach shifts the focus from group-level analyses to individual-level deviations, thereby mitigating confounds arising from intersubject differences to some extent.
29:Complex clinical phenotypes of affective symptoms are not only associated with macroscale functional abnormalities but are also tied to alterations at the microscale genetic and cellular levels (47 -49). Studies of postmortem brain samples and animal models have consistently pointed toward cellular processes in the central nervous system, including pathological alterations of neurons and neuroglial cells, as important factors in the development of affective symptoms (50 -52). However, due to limitations in spatial resolution and coarse diagnostic classi /uniFB01 cations, the relationship between microscopic vulnerabilities, macroscale dysfunctions, and clinical phenotypes remains poorly understood. The Allen Human Brain Atlas (AHBA) microarray dataset has been utilized to investigate transcriptomic patterns linked to neuroimaging /uniFB01 ndings (53,54), with substantial evidence indicating a connection between gene expression and macroscale dysfunctions (55,56). The brainwide gene expression atlas has made it possible to bridge the gap between cross-scale functional alterations (57) and, by combining with a normative modeling framework, could identify the personalized multiscale associations of neural vulnerability.
31:In the current study, we aimed to investigate cross-scale neural dysfunctions related to affective symptoms in patients with MDD, BD, and SZ (Figure 1). By utilizing 2 task-fMRI datasets and a normative modeling framework, robust and individualized functional deviations during emotional memory retrieval were /uniFB01 rst identi /uniFB01 ed. Next, we embedded deviation patterns into macroscale emotional networks (ENs) to explore the functional annotations of emotional pro /uniFB01 ling associated with neural variability in distinct disorders and assessed the associations with affective symptoms dimension. Finally, we conducted transcriptomic association analysis to plausibly determine the cellular abnormalities linked to affective symptoms by integrating macroscale dysfunctions with microscale alterations from the perspective of brainwide transcriptome and brain single-cell sequencing. The normative modeling approach enables the disentangling of the cross-scale heterogeneous patterns of neural dysfunctions underlying affective symptoms within a single diagnosis while simultaneously identifying between-diagnoses commonalities and differences. Building on evidence of common disruption in emotional memory and explicit distinction of affective symptoms across different disorders, we hypothesized that individualized deviations would converge in limited regions but diverge signi /uniFB01 -cantly across large-scale networks between diagnoses.
37:The participants of this study were drawn from 2 datasets. The healthy dataset included 409 healthy control (HC) participants (HCtrain group) recruited from the Center for MRI Research, Peking University. The clinical dataset consisted of 324 individuals: 56 individuals with MDD, 31 individuals with BD, 73 individuals with SZ, and 164 HC participants (HCtest group). These participants were recruited from the Center for Neuroimaging, Peking University Sixth Hospital. The study was approved by the medical ethics committee of Peking University Sixth Hospital (see the Supplement for details of the ethics reviews), and all participants provided written informed consent before taking part in the study. All psychiatric patients were assessed and diagnosed using DSM-IV criteria by experienced psychiatric physicians. The severity of depression in patients with MDD was assessed using the Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (HAMD), while the clinical symptoms of BD were assessed using both the HAMD and the Young Mania Rating Scale. The symptoms of patients with SZ were evaluated using the Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale (PANSS). The HC participants had no current or lifetime history of Axis I psychiatric disorders or neurological disorders. See the Supplement for additional criteria for participants inclusion and exclusion and quality-control details.
41:The fMRI paradigm of emotional episodic memory, which has been used in previous studies (58), was adopted in the healthy and clinical dataset. The paradigm consisted of encoding and retrieval phases, and each phase included neutral and aversive images selected from the International Affective Picture System (59,60). Both encoding and retrieval phases comprised 4 blocks of neutral scenes and 4 blocks of aversive scenes that were continuously presented, and each block included 6 scenes for 3 seconds each scene, alternating with 9 blocks of
